00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.700
So traditionally,
product development teams

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.640
or engineering teams
spend a lot of their time

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.060
designing, developing
new features

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:45.060
for projects and applications.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.010
But once those are ready
to go into production,

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.680
those teams typically
move on to the next thing.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:53.410
And really, the majority
of the total cost

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:56.540
of ownership of software is
after it's in production.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.110
So if you think-- one
month of development time

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:02.230
is really equal to 10 months
of production support.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.500
So if these engineering teams
aren't managing the software

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.060
once it's in production, who is?

00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:13.352
I'm not going to
use this clicker.

00:03:13.352 --> 00:03:14.060
Sorry about that.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.910
All right, so, again,
we have the developers

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:18.590
who are working on
the new features.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.810
And once those are ready
to go into production,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.730
they kind of throw it
over the proverbial wall

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.090
to the operations team.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.950
And the operations team, they
deploy it into production.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.710
But more than deploying
it in production,

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.190
they're the team
that's responsible

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.920
for the reliability,
scalability, and maintenance

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.749
of the software.

00:03:34.749 --> 00:03:36.290
And more than just
the software, it's

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.510
the servers and services and
infrastructure the software

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:40.680
is running on.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.550
And in my experience, there
is a lot more emphasis

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.790
put on hiring for
the development teams

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:47.940
and operations team.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.980
So you get a lot more features
going over to the operations

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.730
team, and the operations
team can't juggle

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:53.680
everything that's going on.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.680
It causes a lot of friction
between these teams,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:56.990
and it's just not scalable.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:00.290
It's a fragile model here.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:09.590
So reducing the product
lifecycle friction.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:10.820
So we start with a business.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.070
A business or product
management teams

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.620
are the ones that make
these feature requests that

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:16.910
go to the development teams.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:19.579
Development teams then
implement these features,

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.350
which then gets handed off
to the operations team.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.440
So you have these barriers
between these teams,

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:25.280
a lot of friction.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:29.360
So we have we have agile
methodology that breaks down

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.330
the barrier between the
business and development teams.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.030
But it doesn't really
help with the barriers

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.410
between the development
and operations team.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.450
So this is where
DevOps comes in.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.760
So we think of DevOps,
really, in five key areas.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.910
First, we want to
break down that wall.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.950
This helps with the
collaboration and velocity

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.800
between the development
and operation team.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.770
Second, we know things
are going to break.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:55.640
We plan for that.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.670
We accept failure as normal.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.060
Two, smaller changes are
easy to implement and deploy.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.090
Which also makes them easier
to rollback, which helps us

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.440
with the meantime to recovery.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.070
Four, we want to remove
human error from the equation

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:10.900
wherever possible.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:14.270
And we do this leveraging,
tooling, and automation.

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:16.100
And fifth, we want to
measure everything,

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.933
because measurement is
critical to determine

00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:20.525
success, especially of
these first four areas.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.720
So the SRE approach
to operations.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.180
We SRE like to treat operations
as an engineering problem.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:34.730
We hire people who don't
want to do repetitive work

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:36.860
and will create
automated solutions.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.920
We use data to guide decisions
and help prioritize what

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.540
we're going to work on next.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:44.540
You can go to the next slide.

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.280
So what do SRE
teams at Google do?

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.450
The SRE teams are not
simply just engineers

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.860
who carry the pagers.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:53.620
SRE folks are
systems engineers who

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.410
develop solutions and
manage large, distributed,

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.255
complex systems at scale.

00:05:58.255 --> 00:06:00.609
The SRE teams at
Google have a saying

00:06:00.609 --> 00:06:02.650
where they like to automate
this year's job away,

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.441
because there are more
interesting problems

00:06:04.441 --> 00:06:07.190
to solve the next year.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:13.180
So if we think of DevOps as a
programming interface, an SRE,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.450
or class SRE, implements DevOps.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.430
DevOps is really a
cultural practice

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:20.410
that breaks down the
silos between not just

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:22.750
the operations and
engineering teams

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:25.165
but your network teams,
your security team,

00:06:25.165 --> 00:06:26.920
the architecture teams.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:29.530
And site reliability
engineering is

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:34.760
a set of those practices we
found to work and a job role.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.880
So now I'm going to
hand this over to Jess

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.102
to talk a little bit about
the key principles of SRE.

