00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.830
So, much of the feedback
loops that drive our paradigm

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.570
have to do with
the error budget.

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:57.850
And we don't expect
you to know what

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.090
this means at all right now.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.480
So we want to,
from the ground up,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.670
establish the significance
of this with you,

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.260
and how we came to
this conclusion.

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:09.190
So first of all, our
goal is reliability.

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:15.460
We need to go through and
determine how we measure this.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.220
The user is the most
important feature in all this.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.770
So the naive approach
doesn't always

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.170
work for depending
on your service,

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:25.260
depending on how
it's distributed.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.870
Good minutes and total minutes
was historically a very common

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:34.120
thing, but this may actually
not be a good representation

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.350
of user happiness.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.880
So instead, we
like to step back--

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.730
ask you all to step back--
and think how the users are

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.840
interacting with the system,
and instead figure out what

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.670
the interaction measurement is.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:55.930
And whether that's queries or
uptime ping or other things,

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.720
if you're more of an MMO.

00:08:00.511 --> 00:08:01.802
ANN WALLACE: Did I go too fast?

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:13.780
JESS FRAME: So this moves
the whole focal point

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.330
of the success measurement
away from the underlying

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.490
individual machines
and code, and off

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:21.730
to what the user actually sees.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:25.330
And we all know whatever
we measure is what

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:26.740
ends up being optimized for.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:30.070
So making sure this
measurement is done well

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:31.360
is the most important thing.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:39.200
So reliability is often
presented as a goal of nines.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.010
You've probably seen
this thing before.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.450
Honestly, it looks
big and scary,

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:48.470
particularly when you get up
to three nines bang nine hours

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.120
of outage here.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:55.430
But even at Google
for a measurement,

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:59.900
five nines is only maintained
by a small number of teams.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.891
This is partly because it takes
a lot of money to achieve that.

00:09:02.891 --> 00:09:05.390
So the underlying infrastructure
is the most important thing

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.070
to get moving with that.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:11.840
But also, honestly, your
individual services,

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:14.660
the reliability of your
ISPs and cellular networks,

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.070
and everything else often
will hit before that,

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.839
and people can't
tell the difference.

00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:20.630
So what about 100%?

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.100
Really, the world
is not about 100%.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.420
Nothing is certain.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.580
Our advice from
running years and years

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:37.030
of large-scale services is,
it's not worth the money.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.070
Its near impossible.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:42.640
Even if we as humanity
had the technology,

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:43.780
it comes at a hefty price.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:47.710
It comes at very slow change and
features, features, features.

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:50.110
That's not something we want.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.870
And each of those nines is
really an exponential cost.

00:09:52.870 --> 00:09:54.379
It's not linear.

00:09:54.379 --> 00:09:56.170
You can spend all of
the money in the world

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.060
trying to make something.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:02.710
Just count the number,
and not to fail ever.

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.100
So we were talking
about availability.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.870
Are SLO-- our Service Level
Objective-- is actually

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:11.820
what we term this internally.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.600
It is our goal.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.200
The main consequence for
this is shame internally,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.020
because we have high
standards for ourselves.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.910
The indicators are the
metrics and other signals

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:28.300
that we use to measure this,
and display it on graphs,

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.130
and do the quarterly reports.

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:35.380
The SLAs end up
being what's often

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.530
shared with external
customers, and the consequences

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:42.100
of that with legal teams
involved tends to be money.

00:10:47.530 --> 00:10:53.630
So again, focusing on user
happiness, getting a good SLO

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:56.010
is not as easy as it seems.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:59.660
But the most important thing to
start with is, keep it simple.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:02.030
And you will iterate
through and get

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.560
something more and more solid.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:09.670
Without backing data,
you revise, you learn.

00:11:09.670 --> 00:11:13.020
That's the only way
to go forward with it.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:14.690
For example, it
may be unintuitive

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:18.510
that you can set a low SLO
and achieve it every day.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.260
But if the user
expects higher of you,

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.720
you will get complaints
before your pager goes off.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.180
And that is a very large issue.

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.540
So vital to the
SRE approach here

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:37.200
is that we get these
three groups in agreement.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:40.100
It's not going to
be easy, but it is--

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.700
without these three being on
the same reliability goal,

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:47.810
and with the promise
to support and defend

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:51.710
each person that's
trying to maintain that,

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:53.012
we can' move forward.

00:11:53.012 --> 00:11:55.220
If we don't have agreement,
if we don't have support,

00:11:55.220 --> 00:11:58.160
it doesn't matter if we're
handed the keys to the system.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:01.640
We won't be able to
work on it properly.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:05.465
It's hard to get this agreement,
but there's luckily a few tools

00:12:05.465 --> 00:12:06.590
that can be used with this.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:08.320
One is pointing out
the exponential cost

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:10.490
to the business people.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:14.240
If they want 100%, that's going
to be very, very expensive.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:17.330
And chances are they don't
want to spend the money on it.

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.760
There's a trade-off between
reliability and speed

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.420
with the developers, and they
probably want to move fast.

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:26.600
And in all of this
system, the users

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.495
will push back with unhappiness
if it is not reliable enough,

00:12:29.495 --> 00:12:30.620
and they will go elsewhere.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:35.680
So this all system will
iterate, but you can get there.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.920
So having decided on
a target and going

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.530
through how we measure it,
and having an agreement

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.680
between the three
groups, we're going

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:49.570
to go back to the error budget.

00:12:49.570 --> 00:12:53.710
Which really is the reverse
of the availability target.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:57.160
And what this is is how
much time you can be down

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.250
and how many requests
you can fail in order

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:06.060
to further getting releases
out, faster change, progress,

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:06.820
all of that.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:08.780
Because we want to build things.

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:11.050
We don't actually
want to say no to you.

00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:13.210
Ever.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:16.240
We have common goals here.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.160
The error budgets luckily
make this nice feedback loop

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:24.310
of having shared goals,
so we have less conflict

00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:25.747
and less churn.

00:13:25.747 --> 00:13:27.080
We're all on the same team here.

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:28.840
We all want a
successful product,

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:31.540
no matter how much
the [INAUDIBLE] people

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:35.520
look like the bad
people to say, no,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.630
we've had too much downtime.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:41.940
You need to cut off and
not do any more releases.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.030
The developers and
others eventually

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:49.110
learn that this is all
for the good of all of us.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:53.730
This whole thing encourages
more responsible expectations

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.590
and velocity as well
from the various teams.

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:02.570
So we were going over the
DevOps principles earlier.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.060
This is kind of how
the error budget

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.520
paradigm fits within them.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.290
So you get the
ownership of the silos.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:12.920
You get the error budgets
themselves, which quantify it,

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.060
which is vital.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:18.137
And how you measure the
reliability is very important.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.870
So the concept of SLO and error
budget are all well and good,

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:30.210
but what do we actually look
like day to day as our job?

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.370
We're going to go over
our areas of expertise

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:36.270
here, although where
the lines are drawn

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:39.670
vary from company to company.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:41.130
I've seen a bit
of variety there.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:48.450
So most of those
are pretty obvious.

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:49.350
Metrics are comment.

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:52.500
Capacity is necessary.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.070
Helping with the
change management.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:56.460
Emergency response is kind
of our bread and butter.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.460
But culture is one of
the interesting ones that

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.190
isn't always pointed out but
is actually the most vital,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:03.840
and everybody is
responsible for it.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:05.430
It's not just the SREs.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.790
Without the culture
being supported

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:18.535
by the business and developers,
it's hard to continue.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:23.520
So monitoring, alerting.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:24.340
Pretty basic.

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:28.530
We do suggest two
different priorities--

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.560
paging and tickets,
depending on how bad

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.860
the system is hurt right
now, or if it's impending,

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.630
to avoid in the future.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:39.970
And the most scary
thing in the world

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:45.700
right there is
noisy urgent alerts.

00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.400
That is where it will
burn out your people.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:52.420
This is what will end up
with your system not getting

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:55.540
the attention that it needs.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:57.550
There's a relevant story
here about crying wolf,

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:00.640
but still, I see
this pattern a lot,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.130
of just a whole bunch
of little alerts

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:07.520
that are treated urgent and the
same level as the whole system

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:08.020
being down.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:12.980
So obviously to
serve traffic, it's

00:16:12.980 --> 00:16:15.767
going to take some resources.

00:16:15.767 --> 00:16:17.600
And one's either going
to have to solve this

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:21.680
with planning your resources
over time, auto scaling,

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.220
or just having absurd
amounts of capacity in case

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:30.170
you have large spikes
to avoid outages.

00:16:30.170 --> 00:16:31.640
The last option
definitely does not

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:37.795
become a possibility the
larger you grow, though.

00:16:37.795 --> 00:16:39.920
Planning becomes more and
more vital at that point.

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:46.370
Even if money isn't an issue,
tracking on the resources

00:16:46.370 --> 00:16:50.090
is absolutely vital so you can
see when something happens,

00:16:50.090 --> 00:16:53.240
if it is a feature
that you want launched,

00:16:53.240 --> 00:16:56.180
and you're acknowledging that
that was a cost you had to it.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:59.910
Or if this is a bug that's just
going to get worse and worse--

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:02.600
a memory leak.

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.910
Different teams handle
this differently.

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:07.880
We prescribed by
rollback immediately

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:10.280
when we see something
bad, and investigate then.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:22.280
So working together with
how changes are introduced.

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.849
Working together with the
development teams often

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:28.970
actually means we can
combine the infrastructure,

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.190
and allow the developers
to move faster.

00:17:32.190 --> 00:17:35.090
Which isn't as intuitive
but there's normally

00:17:35.090 --> 00:17:38.570
a lot of low-hanging fruit
there, as far as things

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:42.300
that will both make the SREs
and the developers happy.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:44.510
Some of those are
progressive rollouts

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:47.600
and all those other things.

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:53.940
A lot of that allows you
to test on a smaller pool

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.136
and confirm before--

00:17:56.136 --> 00:17:58.635
so you can release the faster--

00:17:58.635 --> 00:17:59.760
get the release out faster.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:13.840
So we all want to move faster,
despite common misconceptions

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:14.340
with SRE.

00:18:17.276 --> 00:18:19.710
And the developers can
move as fast as they

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:21.630
want in our paradigm, actually.

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:28.670
The big wall there
is the error budget.

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:32.170
And if the error budget gets
hit, they have to freeze.

00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.330
And they have to freeze
until it's recovered again.

00:18:34.330 --> 00:18:37.780
So most dev teams-- this is
a nice feedback loop of they

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:44.000
prefer to take a partial
hit and not fully freeze

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:46.010
but know that they're
near the budget.

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:47.830
Because if they never
actually exceed,

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:48.830
they don't have to stop.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:50.205
If they exceed,
they have to stop

00:18:50.205 --> 00:18:52.640
until it's recovered again.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.522
If it's a bad enough
outage, it may be a month

00:18:55.522 --> 00:18:57.980
until you actually can start
releasing your features again,

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:00.170
which is something nobody wants.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:08.130
So provisioning.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:09.550
Having the capacity
is one thing.

00:19:09.550 --> 00:19:14.370
Making sure that it
is obtained swiftly,

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.400
and that when it is actually
integrated into the system

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.940
that it doesn't break is
equally important to having it

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.140
in the first place.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.540
If you have an outage because
you added a new region,

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:27.570
and the system
isn't expecting it,

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:30.660
that is possibly
worse than actually

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:32.880
not having the capacity
at all and just dropping

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:33.680
the extra queries.

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:40.690
So what we are known most
for is emergency response.

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:47.110
But surprisingly, this
is actually largely--

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.530
training and support is
responsible for our success.

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:53.080
I've had a lot of
good developers

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:55.930
that were hesitant
to try being on call,

00:19:55.930 --> 00:19:57.061
try the SRE role at all.

00:19:57.061 --> 00:19:58.810
But we have this lovely
program internally

00:19:58.810 --> 00:20:01.570
called mission control,
and we'll bring them over.

00:20:01.570 --> 00:20:04.210
And the majority of them
are able to do on call.

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:05.770
A number of the
most hesitant ones

00:20:05.770 --> 00:20:08.620
actually end up being the
best of our SREs, which

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:10.420
is a really exciting thing see.

00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:13.390
And it's a matter of
not panicking, making

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:16.810
sure we have support,
knowing that you're not going

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.040
to be blamed if you reach out.

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:22.780
The system is more
important than your own ego.

00:20:22.780 --> 00:20:26.560
You're not going to be harmed
financially for it, either.

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:36.540
So starting with the outages
and the postmortems afterwards,

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.840
we need to determine how
bad an outage actually

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:41.940
needs a postmortem.

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:48.660
It's very important to be clear
on consistency of incident size

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:54.260
and the standing definition
for the clarity of all parties

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:55.670
involved on what to expect.

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:04.410
Postmortems are probably the
most vital part of our culture.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:06.720
It's how we learn from
what's happened in the past.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:14.476
It's the most frustrating thing
in the world to repeat outages,

00:21:14.476 --> 00:21:16.350
whether it's the exact
same one or a pattern,

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.150
over and over again.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:20.775
We're here to learn more
things and make it more stable.

00:21:27.789 --> 00:21:29.830
This is one of the big,
scary ones, culture-wise.

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:35.440
This is something
that is hard to do.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.520
It's not easy.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:42.120
Our job is absolutely impossible
without support of our choices,

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:44.200
though, when we're on call.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:45.450
We can be handed a big system.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.699
If we're not handed the equal
trust and responsibility

00:21:47.699 --> 00:21:51.810
to go with that
system, then we are

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:55.110
afraid to make the choices
that are necessary to recover

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:56.160
the system.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.290
We'll instead stick
to what's documented,

00:21:58.290 --> 00:22:01.830
stick to what we know we
won't get in trouble for,

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:04.200
as opposed to taking our
knowledge of the system

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:07.860
and doing what we need
to patch it again.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:11.910
And this is valuable not just
during the outage but at all

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:13.660
times.

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:15.440
Which includes during
the postmortem.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:24.940
Unavoidably, some of our
day-to-day work will be ops.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:27.290
That's how it goes.

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:30.490
We are part software
engineer, we are part systems

00:22:30.490 --> 00:22:32.110
engineer and ops.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:35.870
The important
thing here, though,

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:39.250
is that we need the
support to make choices

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:41.680
on what to automate and when.

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.800
So we can take away all of
these manual, repetitive tasks,

00:22:44.800 --> 00:22:47.551
and instead spend our
time on better things.

00:22:47.551 --> 00:22:50.050
Without that, you're not going
to have a system that scales.

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:54.820
You're just going to throw more
and more people into the bucket

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:56.530
until it actually gets fixed.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:23:02.690
And the best of your
people will end up bored,

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:06.960
which Ann mentioned earlier.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:09.410
We have better problems
to fix next year.

00:23:09.410 --> 00:23:11.510
The team skills.

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:13.670
We like a 50/50 mix.

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:15.950
This often means when
you're converting

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:20.330
from a traditional ops
version to actually keeping

00:23:20.330 --> 00:23:23.059
all of those ops people,
almost all of them

00:23:23.059 --> 00:23:24.350
will be happy to work with you.

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:29.720
And their knowledge is
absolutely invaluable.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:31.280
Everybody should
be able to code.

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:33.740
We do enforce that.

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:37.580
But that is also so that the
system engineers don't end up

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:39.100
with all of the ops work.

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:41.510
We want to be treated as equals.

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:43.196
We want the respect
to be in both ways.

00:23:43.196 --> 00:23:44.570
We don't want to
bucket the work.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:49.642
That creates silos
that are unnecessary.

00:23:49.642 --> 00:23:50.600
It's not very teamlike.

00:23:55.100 --> 00:23:59.570
And again, we will point
out that empowering the SREs

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.120
is the most vital thing
in the world for this.

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:04.250
If you punish the SREs
for doing their job,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:07.310
if you don't give them
the necessary escalation

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:11.270
tools, if you don't back them
when they say this is too much,

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:15.290
the system is
going to fall over,

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:16.760
you're going to have an outage.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:19.110
They are the people who
know most about this,

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:21.380
and you have put
this trust in them.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:28.280
So, a recap of
our SRE principles

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:32.020
and some of the examples
of how that comes in.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:38.640
And again, pulling back
to DevOps with a few

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:43.160
more examples of how we have
the SRE paradigm apply to that.

00:24:46.840 --> 00:24:48.040
ANN WALLACE: Cool.

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.940
So you have all that, right?

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:51.440
So now we're going
to talk about how

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:55.280
you can get started taking
some of what we've talked about

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:58.930
and using it in
your organization.

00:24:58.930 --> 00:24:59.430
Magic.

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:00.025
JESS FRAME: I got it.

00:25:00.025 --> 00:25:02.370
ANN WALLACE: OK, so first,
start with these four things.

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.740
Start with service
level objectives.

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:08.040
SLOs are the combined agreement
between the product, dev,

00:25:08.040 --> 00:25:10.180
and SRE teams.

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:12.150
Without SLOs, you don't
have error budgets.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:15.840
Having both of these
helps prioritize the work.

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:19.400
Two, hire system engineers
who can write software.

00:25:19.400 --> 00:25:21.180
They're going to
end up automating

00:25:21.180 --> 00:25:23.400
a lot of that manual work
that was done traditionally

00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:25.800
by the ops team.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:29.910
Three, treat your
product and operations

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:32.160
or system engineering
teams the same.

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:34.890
We're all software
engineers at the core.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:37.440
We want them to have respect
between the two teams,

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:41.310
not have that wall that you're
just throwing crap over.

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:43.020
And then four, we
want to make sure

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.370
that the SRE teams are
able to choose their work.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:50.180
So for example, if the error
budgets continually go over,

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.600
get exceeded, and the
products engineering team's

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:55.050
not helping out,
then the SRE team

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:59.060
should be able to deprioritize
support for that service.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.020
SREs are valuable
and scarce, so we

00:26:01.020 --> 00:26:03.930
want to use their time wisely.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:05.745
So you've got this.

00:26:05.745 --> 00:26:06.780
Pick one service.

00:26:06.780 --> 00:26:08.280
Pick something simple.

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:09.720
Run it through the SRE model.

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:11.820
Make sure that your
team feels empowered,

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:14.850
that they have support from
management and so forth.

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.100
I mean, you check and make
sure that your services are

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:17.650
up and running.

00:26:17.650 --> 00:26:19.483
Make sure you check in
with the team health.

00:26:19.483 --> 00:26:20.680
Because change is hard.

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:22.230
We all have to adjust.

00:26:22.230 --> 00:26:23.629
Things are going to fail.

00:26:23.629 --> 00:26:25.920
People are going to make
mistakes through this process.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:27.090
That's OK.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:30.420
And using things like the
blameless postmortems helps.

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:31.735
It's an iterative process.

00:26:31.735 --> 00:26:34.110
But once you feel like you've
kind of got this dialed in,

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:36.570
it doesn't have to be perfect.

00:26:36.570 --> 00:26:38.250
Become the internal advocate.

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:40.590
Take that service that you've
run through the SRE model,

00:26:40.590 --> 00:26:42.423
and start talking to
other teams internally.

00:26:42.423 --> 00:26:45.030
You're going to find people
who are excited about this.

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:46.240
And they're are going
to be your people.

00:26:46.240 --> 00:26:48.240
They're going to be your
internal advocate team.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:50.130
And you just keep
talking and showing this,

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:51.671
and you're just
going to start seeing

00:26:51.671 --> 00:26:54.300
some of the SRE practices
pop up in your company.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.550
And it's just going
to continue to evolve.

00:26:56.550 --> 00:26:59.300
And we're here to help.

00:26:59.300 --> 00:27:00.960
So we'll talk about
a few ways that you

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:02.682
can engage with Google.

00:27:02.682 --> 00:27:04.140
JESS FRAME: As I
mentioned earlier,

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:07.470
I am part of the Customer
Reliability team.

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:09.420
We help you learn how
to in your own team.

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:16.500
We'll actually come in and
do workshops, do meetings

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:18.930
where we help you set SLOs,
build your monitoring,

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:22.650
build your SRE team itself.

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:25.080
We are all SREs.

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:28.030
We've all served on
different teams at Google.

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:32.590
So we have some background
on how to get the advocacy,

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:37.210
how to get the different
structures and, of course,

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:41.200
all the technical bits
with postmortems and things

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:44.950
like reel of misfortune,
which is an exercise we do,

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:47.650
running through fake
pages with newbies

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:52.370
so they are more
comfortable with on call.

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:57.260
So, how to get us a CRE is
a little more interesting,

00:27:57.260 --> 00:27:59.660
though, because there is
not a lot of us right now.

00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:04.100
There's 20, 30 of us
worldwide, somewhere in there.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:05.600
So you're going to
contact your TAM,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:09.640
but you've got to keep
in mind your executives,

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:13.990
your C levels need to
be behind the process.

00:28:13.990 --> 00:28:17.440
Because again, we were talking
about the culture support.

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:20.079
Ultimately, if
somebody is not happy,

00:28:20.079 --> 00:28:22.120
they're going to come and
escalate, and escalate,

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:26.200
and they have to be supported.

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:28.240
And interestingly,
CRE is a group

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:31.900
that you don't pay
for the services.

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:35.670
Partly because of our
expertise, partly because we're

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:37.420
a scarce resource.

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:40.780
But you do pay with your
willingness and time.

00:28:40.780 --> 00:28:43.256
So we do expect you
to have homework

00:28:43.256 --> 00:28:44.380
with each of the workshops.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:46.296
Like, we may help you
build a few of the SLOs.

00:28:46.296 --> 00:28:47.830
We're going to
expect the rest back.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:50.500
We're going to expect to
see you building the metrics

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:54.370
and showing us the graphs, so we
know that you're there with us

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:57.780
and ready for the next step.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:28:59.950
ANN WALLACE: Cool, so
there's also a Professional

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:03.210
Services, a group I'm part of.

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:05.770
We're a paid
service, but we come

00:29:05.770 --> 00:29:08.740
in-- we can help
organizations by doing intro

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:12.220
to SRE workshops, or more
advanced SLO workshops.

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:13.750
So you pick a
couple of services,

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:16.300
and we run through
some of the SRE models

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:18.820
and help you to find
those SLOs, and what

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:21.820
would happen if your error
budget gets exceeded,

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:22.810
and so forth.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.750
And it also could be
companies that might not

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:28.060
meet the criteria yet for CREs.

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:29.480
We could help with that.

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:33.740
And then maybe eventually
get people like Jess help.

00:29:33.740 --> 00:29:41.470
So, again, you can contact your
sales or your TAMs, or ping us.

00:29:41.470 --> 00:29:42.730
We also have books.

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:45.344
So we have the "Site Reliability
Engineering" book that's

00:29:45.344 --> 00:29:46.510
been out for a little while.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:50.270
The workbook that's
just been released.

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:52.530
JESS FRAME: We've got some
launch day copies over

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:54.340
at the five nines.

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:55.120
So come visit us.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.500
We've got a bunch of CREs there.

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:59.440
A few SREs from
other teams as well.

00:29:59.440 --> 00:30:03.970
And if you'd like to start
reading that before you engage

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:08.050
us, that is an opportunity to
see if you are on the same page

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.640
and want to execute this.

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:12.850
ANN WALLACE: And "Seeking
SRE" is coming soon.

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:13.570
Thank you all.

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:16.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

